{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients with higher tumor mutation burden (TMB) exhibit increased expression of immune checkpoint genes PDCD1 and CTLA4.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify TMB per patient and test its association with PDCD1 and CTLA4 expression levels in SKCM tumors.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv. Filter to high‐impact coding variants (e.g., Variant_Classification in {\"Missense_Mutation\",\"Nonsense_Mutation\",\"Splice_Site\",\"Frame_Shift_Del\",\"Frame_Shift_Ins\"}).\\n2. Count mutations per patient_id to derive TMB.\\n3. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; locate Ensembl IDs for PDCD1 and CTLA4 (e.g., search expr.index.str.contains).\\n4. Extract expression rows, transpose, map sample barcodes → patient_id, and average expression per patient.\\n5. Merge TMB and checkpoint expression by patient_id.\\n6. Compute Pearson and Spearman correlations between TMB and each gene.\\n7. Fit multivariate linear regressions: PDCD1_expr ~ TMB + age_at_diagnosis + gender and CTLA4_expr ~ TMB + age + gender.\\n8. Check residual diagnostics and adjust for multiple testing if necessary.","deliverables":"• Table of TMB, PDCD1_expr, CTLA4_expr per patient.\\n• Correlation coefficients and p‐values for TMB vs PDCD1 and CTLA4.\\n• Regression summaries (coefficients, p‐values, R²).\\n• Diagnostic plots (residuals QQ‐plot, residual vs fitted).  "  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Presence of the BRAF V600E mutation is associated with upregulation of MAPK‐pathway feedback genes DUSP6, ETV5, and FOS in melanoma tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare expression of MAPK feedback genes between BRAF V600E‐mutant and wildtype SKCM tumors.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv; filter rows where Hugo_Symbol=='BRAF' and HGVSp_Short contains 'V600E'. Label those patient_ids as BRAF_V600E = 1, others as 0.\\n2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; identify Ensembl IDs for DUSP6, ETV5, and FOS via index.str.contains on gene symbols.\\n3. Extract, transpose, map to patient_id, and average expression per gene per patient.\\n4. Merge expression of DUSP6, ETV5, FOS with BRAF_V600E label.\\n5. For each gene, compare expression in mutant vs wildtype using Wilcoxon rank‐sum test (or t‐test if normal).\\n6. Adjust p‐values across the three tests (e.g., Benjamini‐Hochberg).\\n7. Visualize boxplots for each gene by mutation status.","deliverables":"• Dataset of BRAF_V600E status and DUSP6, ETV5, FOS expressions.\\n• Test statistics and adjusted p‐values for each gene.\\n• Boxplots for expression differences.  "  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Increasing Breslow thickness is positively correlated with activation of an epithelial‐to‐mesenchymal transition (EMT) gene signature (VIM, SNAI1, CDH2).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the association between histologic Breslow thickness and EMT signature expression in melanoma.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; extract patient_id and breslow_thickness, drop missing.\\n2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; find Ensembl IDs for VIM, SNAI1, CDH2.\\n3. Extract, transpose, map to patient_id, average per patient.\\n4. For each gene, compute z‐scores across patients and derive a composite EMT score by averaging the three z‐scores.\\n5. Merge Breslow thickness and EMT score by patient_id.\\n6. Plot Breslow vs EMT score; compute Pearson and Spearman correlations.\\n7. Fit linear regression: EMT_score ~ breslow_thickness + age_at_diagnosis + gender; perform residual diagnostics.","deliverables":"• Merged table of breslow_thickness and EMT_score.\\n• Correlation results with p‐values.\\n• Regression summary and diagnostic plots.\\n• Scatter plot with regression line.  "  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher tumor mutation burden is associated with reduced overall survival in SKCM patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether TMB predicts overall survival time adjusting for clinical covariates.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv; count non‐synonymous mutations per patient_id to compute TMB.\\n2. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; derive survival time = days_to_death if available, else days_to_last_followup, and event indicator (1=death, 0=censored). Extract age_at_diagnosis, gender, and AJCC pathologic stage.\\n3. Merge TMB with survival data by patient_id.\\n4. Use a Cox proportional hazards model (e.g., lifelines CoxPHFitter) with Surv(time,event) ~ TMB + age + gender + stage.\\n5. Check proportional hazards assumption.\\n6. Generate Kaplan–Meier curves stratified by TMB tertiles and log‐rank test.","deliverables":"• Cox model summary (hazard ratios, 95% CI, p‐values).\\n• PH assumption test results.\\n• Kaplan–Meier plots by TMB tertiles with survival curves and log‐rank p‐value.\\n• Dataset with survival, event, TMB, and covariates.  "  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients with 'WITH TUMOR' baseline neoplasm status have lower tumor CD8A expression compared to those 'TUMOR FREE' at baseline.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare expression of the CD8+ T‐cell marker CD8A between baseline disease groups.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; filter non‐missing baseline_neoplasm_status and label patients as 'TUMOR_FREE' or 'WITH_TUMOR'.\\n2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; locate Ensembl ID(s) for CD8A.\\n3. Extract, transpose, map to patient_id, average expression per patient.\\n4. Merge CD8A_expr with baseline status by patient_id.\\n5. Compare distributions via Wilcoxon test (or t‐test) and plot violin/boxplots.\\n6. Fit linear model: CD8A_expr ~ baseline_status + age + gender and check residuals.","deliverables":"• Table of baseline_status and CD8A_expr.\\n• Test statistic and p‐value.\\n• Boxplot/violin plot of CD8A expression by group.\\n• Regression summary and diagnostic plots.  "  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Ulcerated melanoma tumors exhibit higher expression of angiogenesis genes VEGFA, ANGPT2, and TEK compared to non‐ulcerated tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether histologic ulceration is linked to upregulation of angiogenesis markers.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; filter ulceration_indicator in {\"YES\",\"NO\"} and label accordingly.\\n2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; identify Ensembl IDs for VEGFA, ANGPT2, TEK.\\n3. Extract, transpose, map to patient_id, average expression per gene.\\n4. Compute an angiogenesis score as the mean z‐score of the three genes per patient.\\n5. Merge ulceration label and angiogenesis score by patient_id.\\n6. Compare scores between ulcerated vs non‐ulcerated using Wilcoxon test; plot boxplots.\\n7. Fit regression: angiogenesis_score ~ ulceration + age + gender.","deliverables":"• Dataset with ulceration status and angiogenesis_score.\\n• Test results (statistic, p‐value).\\n• Boxplot of scores by ulceration.\\n• Regression output and diagnostics.  "  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tumors harboring truncating mutations in MUC16 have reduced MUC16 mRNA expression compared to wildtype tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine if truncating MUC16 mutations lead to decreased transcript levels of MUC16.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv; filter Hugo_Symbol=='MUC16' and Variant_Classification in {\"Nonsense_Mutation\",\"Frame_Shift_Del\",\"Frame_Shift_Ins\",\"Splice_Site\"}. Label those patient_ids as MUT_trunc = 1, others as 0.\\n2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; find versioned Ensembl ID for MUC16.\\n3. Extract, transpose, map to patient_id, average MUC16_expr.\\n4. Merge expression with MUT_trunc by patient_id.\\n5. Compare MUC16_expr in mutant vs wildtype via Wilcoxon test and visualize with boxplot.\\n6. Optionally adjust for tumor purity proxies if available.","deliverables":"• Table of MUC16 truncation status and expression.\\n• Wilcoxon test result and p‐value.\\n• Boxplot comparing MUC16 expression.  "  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients with prior radiation therapy exhibit elevated expression of DNA damage response (DDR) genes ATM, TP53BP1, and BRCA1 in their tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether clinical history of radiation is reflected in upregulation of DDR pathway genes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; filter prior_radiation_therapy in {\"YES\",\"NO\"} and label accordingly.\\n2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; identify Ensembl IDs for ATM, TP53BP1, BRCA1.\\n3. Extract, transpose, map to patient_id, average expression per gene.\\n4. Compute a composite DDR score as the mean z‐score of the three genes.\\n5. Merge DDR score with radiation history by patient_id.\\n6. Compare DDR scores between groups via Wilcoxon test; plot boxplots.\\n7. Fit regression: DDR_score ~ prior_radiation_therapy + age + gender.","deliverables":"• Dataset of prior_radiation_therapy and DDR_score.\\n• Test statistics and p‐value for group difference.\\n• Boxplots of DDR scores.\\n• Regression summary and diagnostics.  "  
    }  
  }  
]}
